    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d743">

                <pb facs="1029" n="1019" xml:id="pg1019"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by General Marshall to the Minister-Counselor
                        of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Butterworth</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">OSE 555</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-11-05">November 5,
                        1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dr. Stuart showed me a copy of the letter<anchor n="60" xml:id="d743-f1"/> which
                    you referred to me and which is returned herewith. I told Dr. Stuart that I did
                    not think that the U. S. would be favorably inclined to extend further credit
                    for cotton at this time.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d743-f2"/> It was difficult to
                    obtain even the 30 million credit at the first of this year. I told him that I
                    would like to have Mr. Adler’s comments before considering the matter
                    further.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
